e in their initial thought.\n\nLet me confirm using another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n```\n     558\n   \u00d7 192\n   -------\n     1116   (558 \u00d7 2)\n    50220   (558 \u00d7 90)\n   558000   (558 \u00d7 100)\n   ---------\n   107136\n```\n\nAdding these together:\n\n1,116 + 50,220 = 51,336\n\n51,336 + 558,000 = 609,336\n\nWait, now I'm confused. There's a conflict between the two methods. Let me check my calculations again.\n\nWait, no, when multiplying 558 by 192, the correct way is:\n\n558 \u00d7 192 = 558 \u00d7 (200 - 8) = 558 \u00d7 200 - 558 \u00d7 8 = 111,600 - 4,464 = 107,136.\n\nAlternatively, breaking it down:\n\n558 \u00d7 192 = 558 \u00d7 (100 + 90 + 2) = 558\u00d7100 + 558\u00d790 + 558\u00d72 = 55,800 + 50,220 + 1,116 = 107,136.\n\nSo, the correct product is 107,136.\n\nThe user's initial thought of 607,136 is incorrect. It seems they might have added an extra digit or made a place value error.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 558 and 192 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. Multiply 558 by 2: 558 \u00d7 2 = 1,116\n2. Multiply 558 by 90: 558 \u00d7 90 = 50,220\n3. Multiply 558 by 100: 558 \u00d7 100 = 55,800\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n1,116 + 50,220 = 51,336  \n51,336 + 55,800 = 107,136\n\nSo, the final computed product is **107,136**.",
